import LRSpectrumFull.Sext6.Gap.Q81S2

/-!
# Q8.1, Theorem C for `N = 5..9`, both multipliers large: the 23 coprime pairs  [2026-11-25]

Source: the accompanying notes on Zhang's Question 8.1 section 3, Lemma 4 (the cases `N <= 9` that the counting
inequality `(*)` does not cover), made uniform in `(m1, m2)` by `Q81S2.cover_all`.
For each coprime pair `N/2 < r1 < r2 <= N`, `5 <= N <= 9`, four finite checks (`decide +kernel`, no `native_decide`)
establish `covP` for ALL `m1 >= r2`, `m2 >= r1`; then `1/N <= ML (v2Fam k r1 r2 m1 m2)`.
The box sizes, the explicit rational times `η = p/q` and the corner `(P, Q)` are generated by an exact-integer script
(the generator script of the accompanying notes on Question 8.1); Lean does not trust the script, it re-checks every finite statement.
* `theoremC_five_nine` : the full statement for `N = 5..9`.
No `sorry`, no `axiom`.

Convention: `N = k + 1` is the number of speeds of the vector `v : Fin (k+1) → ℤ`. Speeds are the moving runners; the runner at rest is not counted, so a vector of `N` speeds means `N + 1` runners and the Lonely Runner value is `1/(N+1)`.
Hypotheses: those of the individual statements (numerical bounds, structural conditions and, where named in the list above, window predicates such as `PrimeWin`, `CopWin`, `PrimeWindow7`, which later modules discharge for explicit ranges). The unproved predicates `DusartPi`, `LRCHyp`, `CopWinHyp` are not hypotheses of any statement in this module.
Axiom audit: covered by `Axioms22` (`#print axioms`; declarations not printed there are bricks of printed ones, so their axioms are bounded by the printed lines).
-/

